ALL = []
A = ALL.append

A(('h1', 'What You Will Learn in This Study'))
A(('p', 'By the end of this study, you should be able to:'))
A(('bul', [
    'Trace the markers that open each part of 1 Corinthians 7 (“Now concerning,” “by permission,” “I command,” “I speak,” “my judgment”) and note whose word each marker says an instruction is.',
    'Read verses 1–9 in order and record what is said of the husband and the wife, of consent and of the unmarried and widows, using the verbs the passage uses.',
    'Set 1 Corinthians 7:10–11 beside Matthew 19:3–9 and Mark 10:2–12 and list, point by point, where the wording agrees, where it differs and what each passage says about its hearers.',
    'Follow verses 12–16 and mark what the passage states about a believer married to an unbeliever, and what it leaves unsaid.',
    'Observe the three statements of one rule in verses 17–24 and the question form that returns in verse 27, without carrying the rule beyond the examples the chapter gives.',
    'Test three common readings of the chapter against its own words, sorting what the text states from what readers add.',
]))
A(('call', 'Study Goal',
   'To read 1 Corinthians 7 from its first verse to its last, in the order Paul wrote it, and to set down what each instruction says, to whom it is addressed, and whose word Paul says it is, so that every reading rests on what the chapter states and goes no further.'))
A(('nav', 'Study Outline'))
A(('num', [
    'The Setting: a letter answering a letter, and the markers that divide the chapter.',
    'Verses 1–9: what is said of duty, consent, the unmarried and widows.',
    'Verses 10–11: the command Paul gives and the Gospel passages set beside it.',
    'Verses 12–16: the word to the rest, and what it states about a mixed household.',
    'Verses 17–24: one rule in three statements, with circumcision and bondage as examples.',
    'Verses 25–38: virgins, the present distress and the time that is short.',
    'Verses 39–40 and the Chapter Side by Side: the widow, the two tables and three readings tested.',
    'Conclusion, Study Summary, Key Distinctions, Scripture-Tracing, Review, Teaching Outline, Guided Further Study and Final Synthesis.',
]))

A(('h1', 'Introduction'))
A(('p', 'First Corinthians 7 is read quickly and carried far. A reader meets “It is good for a man not to touch a woman” or “let him not put her away” and the mind moves at once to rules for every marriage and every unmarried person. This study slows that movement. It reads the chapter in the order Paul wrote it, asks of each instruction whom Paul is addressing and whose word he says it is, and records what the chapter leaves unsaid.'))
A(('p', 'The study does not read the whole teaching of Scripture on marriage. Study 48 (The Christian Household) reads the instruction to husbands and wives in Ephesians 5 and Colossians 3. Study 1 (The Bride of Christ) reads Paul’s marriage imagery. Studies 36, 72 and 81 take other parts of this same letter. This study asks one narrower thing: what does 1 Corinthians 7 say to the church at Corinth about marriage and staying single, on whose authority does each instruction rest, and what does it leave unsaid?'))
A(('p', 'Two habits govern the work. First, each instruction is read in its own place, with its speaker, its audience and the words that mark its authority. Second, a link between passages is made only where a passage makes it. When the chapter leaves a matter unsaid, the study records that and stops.'))

A(('h1', S1))
A(('p', '1.1 To whom the letter goes. The letter opens “Unto the church of God which is at Corinth, to them that are sanctified in Christ Jesus, called to be saints, with all that in every place call upon the name of Jesus Christ our Lord, both theirs and ours” (1:2). Chapter 7 is part of that letter and speaks to those it names. This reading rests on the passage’s own audience markers and setting.'))
A(('p', '1.2 A letter answering a letter. The chapter begins by telling its hearers what it answers: “Now concerning the things whereof ye wrote unto me” (7:1). Paul is replying to something the Corinthians wrote. The same form returns at 7:25 (“Now concerning virgins”) and again in this letter, at 8:1 and 12:1. The letter also refers to an earlier letter from Paul (5:9). The table sets the first verses side by side.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ('1 Cor 1:2', '“Unto the church of God which is at Corinth, to them that are sanctified in Christ Jesus, called to be saints”', 'The address. The audience is named in the first verse of the letter.'),
    ('1 Cor 5:9', '“I wrote unto you in an epistle not to company with fornicators”', 'Paul refers to an earlier letter. The chapter under study is not the first thing he wrote to them.'),
    ('1 Cor 7:1', '“Now concerning the things whereof ye wrote unto me”', 'The opening of the chapter names its occasion: what the Corinthians wrote.'),
    ('1 Cor 7:25', '“Now concerning virgins”', 'The same form opens a second answer inside the chapter.'),
], [0.15, 0.48, 0.37]))
A(('p', '1.3 What the markers do. Inside the chapter Paul tells his hearers, again and again, what kind of word he is giving. He speaks “by permission, and not of commandment” (7:6). He commands, and says whose command it is: “yet not I, but the Lord” (7:10). He speaks, and says it is “not the Lord” (7:12). He has “no commandment of the Lord” and gives “my judgment” (7:25). He closes “after my judgment” (7:40). These markers divide the chapter, and this study follows them: each section below begins where one of them stands.'))
A(('call', 'The Question Asked of Every Instruction',
   'Of each instruction the study asks four things: who is addressed, what is said, whose word Paul says it is, and what the passage leaves unsaid. The chapter itself supplies the third answer, and the study does not turn a statement of authority into a rule about the weight of the instruction.'))

A(('h1', S2))
A(('p', '2.1 The chapter’s opening sentence. “It is good for a man not to touch a woman” (7:1) is printed in the KJV as one sentence after the colon that follows “wrote unto me.” The text does not mark whether the words are the Corinthians’ own or Paul’s. The study does not decide; it records that what follows in verse 2 begins “Nevertheless.”'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ('1 Cor 7:1–2', '“It is good for a man not to touch a woman. Nevertheless, to avoid fornication, let every man have his own wife, and let every woman have her own husband.”', 'A statement, then a limit: “Nevertheless.” The reason given is “to avoid fornication,” and the instruction runs to “every man” and “every woman.”'),
    ('1 Cor 7:3–4', '“Let the husband render unto the wife due benevolence: and likewise also the wife unto the husband. The wife hath not power of her own body, but the husband: and likewise also the husband hath not power of his own body, but the wife.”', 'The duty is stated for each partner, and each statement is followed by “likewise also.” Neither is spoken to alone.'),
    ('1 Cor 7:5', '“Defraud ye not one the other, except it be with consent for a time, that ye may give yourselves to fasting and prayer; and come together again, that Satan tempt you not for your incontinency.”', 'One exception is stated, with its conditions: consent, a time, a purpose and a return.'),
    ('1 Cor 7:6–7', '“But I speak this by permission, and not of commandment. For I would that all men were even as I myself. But every man hath his proper gift of God, one after this manner, and another after that.”', 'The first authority marker. Paul states what he would wish and then states that each man has “his proper gift of God.”'),
    ('1 Cor 7:8–9', '“I say therefore to the unmarried and widows, It is good for them if they abide even as I. But if they cannot contain, let them marry: for it is better to marry than to burn.”', 'A word to two groups, “the unmarried and widows,” with a wish and a limit stated together.'),
], [0.15, 0.48, 0.37]))
A(('p', '2.2 Verses 2–4: duty on both sides. The reason for verse 2 is stated in the verse: “to avoid fornication.” The words “his own wife” and “her own husband” belong to the same sentence. Verses 3 and 4 then give the duty and the claim as two matched statements, one for each partner.'))
A(('p', '2.3 Verse 5: the one exception. Verse 5 states that an exception is allowed “with consent for a time,” names its purpose (“fasting and prayer”) and requires that the two “come together again.” It also states a reason: “that Satan tempt you not for your incontinency.” The verse does not say how long the time may be, and it does not say how often an exception may be made.'))
A(('p', '2.4 Verses 6–7: permission and gift. “This” in “I speak this by permission” can look back to verse 2, to verses 2–5 or to verse 1; the text does not say which, and the study does not decide. The statement that follows is Paul’s wish that “all men were even as I myself,” set at once beside the statement that “every man hath his proper gift of God.” The passage does not name what the gift is. It says that the gifts differ: “one after this manner, and another after that.”'))
A(('p', '2.5 Verses 8–9: the unmarried and widows. Paul tells “the unmarried and widows” that it is “good” for them to “abide even as I.” The next sentence qualifies it: “But if they cannot contain, let them marry: for it is better to marry than to burn.” The text does not define “burn.” The chapter does not say whether Paul was ever married, and the study does not supply it. In another chapter of the same letter he writes “Have we not power to lead about a sister, a wife, as well as other apostles” (9:5); that sentence asks about a power and does not state Paul’s own situation.'))

A(('h1', S3))
A(('p', '3.1 The marker. The first verses of this section carry the strongest marker in the chapter: “And unto the married I command, yet not I, but the Lord” (7:10). Paul gives a command and says it is not his own but the Lord’s. The content follows: “Let not the wife depart from her husband: But and if she depart, let her remain unmarried, or be reconciled to her husband: and let not the husband put away his wife” (7:10–11).'))
A(('p', '3.2 What the verses say. Three things are stated. The wife is not to depart from her husband. If she does depart, she is to “remain unmarried” or be “reconciled.” The husband is not to “put away his wife.” The verbs differ for the two partners: the wife is told not to depart, the husband not to put away. The passage names no exception and does not mention remarriage except in the word “unmarried.”'))
A(('p', '3.3 The Lord’s words in the Gospels. The Gospels record two occasions on which the Lord was asked about putting away a wife, and each passage names its setting. In Matthew 19:3 “the Pharisees … came unto him, tempting him, and saying unto him, Is it lawful for a man to put away his wife for every cause?” In Mark 10:2 “the Pharisees came to him, and asked him, Is it lawful for a man to put away his wife? tempting him.” The table sets the three passages side by side.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ('1 Cor 7:10–11', '“And unto the married I command, yet not I, but the Lord, Let not the wife depart from her husband: But and if she depart, let her remain unmarried, or be reconciled to her husband: and let not the husband put away his wife.”', 'Paul’s hearers: “the married.” The verb for the wife is depart; for the husband, put away. Remaining unmarried or reconciliation is stated for the wife who departs. No exception is stated.'),
    ('Matt 19:6–9', '“What therefore God hath joined together, let not man put asunder. … Moses because of the hardness of your hearts suffered you to put away your wives: but from the beginning it was not so. And I say unto you, Whosoever shall put away his wife, except it be for fornication, and shall marry another, committeth adultery.”', 'The Lord speaks to Pharisees who are “tempting him.” He answers from “the beginning” (19:4–5) and from Moses (19:7–8). An exception is stated: “except it be for fornication.”'),
    ('Mark 10:9–12', '“What therefore God hath joined together, let not man put asunder. … Whosoever shall put away his wife, and marry another, committeth adultery against her. And if a woman shall put away her husband, and be married to another, she committeth adultery.”', 'The Lord speaks first to Pharisees (10:2) and then “in the house” to his disciples (10:10). No exception is stated, and a woman who puts away her husband is addressed (10:12).'),
], [0.15, 0.48, 0.37]))
A(('p', '3.4 Where the wording agrees and differs. The two Gospel passages share the saying “What therefore God hath joined together, let not man put asunder”; 1 Corinthians 7 does not quote it. All three passages share a concern with a man putting away his wife. They differ in their hearers (the married at Corinth; Pharisees, and in Mark the disciples in the house), in the verbs used for the wife and the husband, in the exception (stated in Matthew 19:9, absent from Mark 10:11 and from 1 Corinthians 7:10–11) and in what is said of a wife who has left (reconciled or unmarried in 7:11; no such statement in the Gospels).'))
A(('p', '3.5 What the study does not claim. The chapter does not name the saying to which “the Lord” refers, and it does not quote a Gospel. The study does not say that 7:10 is a citation of Matthew 19 or Mark 10, and it does not say it is not. It draws no rule about divorce and remarriage from the three passages, and it does not decide whether the exception in Matthew 19:9 governs 7:10–11. A fuller treatment of the Lord’s words in the Sermon on the Mount (Matthew 5:31–32) belongs to its own setting and is not taken up here.'))

A(('h1', S4))
A(('p', '4.1 The marker. “But to the rest speak I, not the Lord” (7:12). The word is Paul’s, and the verse says so. “The rest” follows “the married” (7:10); the case that follows is stated in the verse itself: “If any brother hath a wife that believeth not.” The text does not say why this case is introduced by “not the Lord,” and it does not say Paul had no word from the Lord on the matter, only that the word given is his.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ('1 Cor 7:12–13', '“But to the rest speak I, not the Lord: If any brother hath a wife that believeth not, and she be pleased to dwell with him, let him not put her away. And the woman which hath an husband that believeth not, and if he be pleased to dwell with her, let her not leave him.”', 'The case is a believer with an unbelieving spouse. Each instruction is conditioned: “be pleased to dwell.”'),
    ('1 Cor 7:14', '“For the unbelieving husband is sanctified by the wife, and the unbelieving wife is sanctified by the husband: else were your children unclean; but now are they holy.”', 'A reason is given with “For.” The words “sanctified,” “unclean” and “holy” are used and are not defined in the verse.'),
    ('1 Cor 7:15', '“But if the unbelieving depart, let him depart. A brother or a sister is not under bondage in such cases: but God hath called us to peace.”', 'A second case: the unbelieving spouse departs. The statement is limited by the words “in such cases.” The reason offered is “God hath called us to peace.”'),
    ('1 Cor 7:16', '“For what knowest thou, O wife, whether thou shalt save thy husband? or how knowest thou, O man, whether thou shalt save thy wife?”', 'Two questions, one to each partner. The verse does not answer them.'),
], [0.15, 0.48, 0.37]))
A(('p', '4.2 Verses 12–13: the instruction. Both instructions are conditional. The believing husband is not to put away a wife “that believeth not” if “she be pleased to dwell with him”; the believing wife is not to leave a husband “that believeth not” if “he be pleased to dwell with her.” The passage does not address a case in which the unbeliever is not pleased to stay until verse 15.'))
A(('p', '4.3 Verse 14: the reason. Verse 14 states a reason, introduced by “For.” It says the unbelieving husband “is sanctified by the wife,” the unbelieving wife “by the husband,” and that “your children” are “holy” where they would otherwise be “unclean.” The verse does not say that the unbelieving spouse is saved, and verse 16 asks that very question of the believer: “whether thou shalt save thy husband.” The study does not define “sanctified,” “unclean” or “holy” beyond how the verse uses them.'))
A(('p', '4.4 Verse 15: the departure. “If the unbelieving depart, let him depart.” The next sentence states that “a brother or a sister is not under bondage in such cases.” The chapter does not say here what the believer is released from beyond the words “in such cases,” and it does not mention remarriage in verses 12–16. Verse 11 speaks of remaining unmarried and verse 39 of being “at liberty to be married,” each in its own setting; the study does not carry either into verse 15. The sentence closes with its reason: “God hath called us to peace.”'))
A(('p', '4.5 Verse 16: the open question. The two questions in verse 16 each begin “what knowest thou” and “how knowest thou.” The verse does not say whether the questions express hope or doubt; the same words are open to both. The study notes the two possibilities and does not choose between them.'))

A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', '5.1 One rule, stated three times. The rule appears in verse 17, again in verse 20 and a third time in verse 24, each time in different words. The table sets the three statements beside the examples the chapter gives.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ('1 Cor 7:17', '“But as God hath distributed to every man, as the Lord hath called every one, so let him walk. And so ordain I in all churches.”', 'The rule in its widest form: each man walks as called. Paul states that he gives the same order “in all churches.”'),
    ('1 Cor 7:18–19', '“Is any man called being circumcised? let him not become uncircumcised. Is any called in uncircumcision? let him not be circumcised. Circumcision is nothing, and uncircumcision is nothing, but the keeping of the commandments of God.”', 'The first example, with its reason. The verse does not say which commandments, and it states the matter in the form of a question and its answer.'),
    ('1 Cor 7:20–21', '“Let every man abide in the same calling wherein he was called. Art thou called being a servant? care not for it: but if thou mayest be made free, use it rather.”', 'The rule restated, then a second example. The example ends with a choice the text states: “if thou mayest be made free, use it rather.”'),
    ('1 Cor 7:22–24', '“For he that is called in the Lord, being a servant, is the Lord’s freeman: likewise also he that is called, being free, is Christ’s servant. Ye are bought with a price; be not ye the servants of men. Brethren, let every man, wherein he is called, therein abide with God.”', 'The reason given for the examples, and the rule a third time. “Ye are bought with a price” stands inside the instruction.'),
], [0.15, 0.48, 0.37]))
A(('p', '5.2 What the first statement says. The rule in verse 17 is attached to what God and the Lord have done: “as God hath distributed,” “as the Lord hath called.” The phrase “so ordain I in all churches” states that Paul gives this order everywhere; it does not say what the order covers beyond the words of the verse and the examples that follow.'))
A(('p', '5.3 Circumcision (7:18–19). The two questions in verse 18 run in opposite directions: the circumcised is not to become uncircumcised, and the uncircumcised is not to be circumcised. Verse 19 then states that “circumcision is nothing, and uncircumcision is nothing, but the keeping of the commandments of God.” The verse does not say which commandments are meant. Study 51 reads circumcision as a sign and its meaning in its own passages, and Study 59 asks what the Law’s jurisdiction is; this study records verse 19 as written and does not answer either question.'))
A(('p', '5.4 Bondage (7:20–24). The example of the servant is stated with a limit and a permission: “care not for it: but if thou mayest be made free, use it rather.” The following verses give the reason in two statements, one for the servant and one for the freeman: the first “is the Lord’s freeman,” the second “is Christ’s servant.” Then comes “Ye are bought with a price; be not ye the servants of men.” The same words appear in 6:20, “For ye are bought with a price: therefore glorify God in your body,” in a different argument. Study 53 asks what redemption is and what price is meant; this study does not take that up and sets the two verses side by side only as written.'))
A(('p', '5.5 How the section stands in the chapter. Verses 17–24 do not mention marriage, but the question form used for circumcision and bondage (“Is any man called being circumcised?” “Art thou called being a servant?”) returns in verse 27 (“Art thou bound unto a wife?”). The text does not say that the rule of 7:17–24 governs 7:25–38 or that it was written to do so. The study records that the form is shared and does not state a connection the chapter does not state.'))

A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', '6.1 The marker. “Now concerning virgins I have no commandment of the Lord: yet I give my judgment, as one that hath obtained mercy of the Lord to be faithful” (7:25). The matter is named (“virgins”), the Lord’s commandment is said to be absent, and Paul gives “my judgment.” The words “as one that hath obtained mercy of the Lord to be faithful” state the standing from which he gives it.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ('1 Cor 7:26–28', '“I suppose therefore that this is good for the present distress, I say, that it is good for a man so to be. Art thou bound unto a wife? seek not to be loosed. Art thou loosed from a wife? seek not a wife. But and if thou marry, thou hast not sinned; and if a virgin marry, she hath not sinned. Nevertheless such shall have trouble in the flesh: but I spare you.”', 'A supposition tied to “the present distress,” two paired commands, then the statement that marriage is not sin. Trouble “in the flesh” is named, and Paul says he spares them.'),
    ('1 Cor 7:29–31', '“But this I say, brethren, the time is short: it remaineth, that both they that have wives be as though they had none; And they that weep, as though they wept not; and they that rejoice, as though they rejoiced not; and they that buy, as though they possessed not; And they that use this world, as not abusing it: for the fashion of this world passeth away.”', 'Five pairs set “as though.” The reason stated is “the time is short” and “the fashion of this world passeth away.”'),
    ('1 Cor 7:32–35', '“But I would have you without carefulness. He that is unmarried careth for the things that belong to the Lord, how he may please the Lord: But he that is married careth for the things that are of the world, how he may please his wife. … And this I speak for your own profit; not that I may cast a snare upon you, but for that which is comely, and that ye may attend upon the Lord without distraction.”', 'Paul states his wish, the difference in what each cares for, and the purpose he gives: “your own profit,” “comely,” “without distraction.” He says it is not “a snare.”'),
    ('1 Cor 7:36–38', '“But if any man think that he behaveth himself uncomely toward his virgin, if she pass the flower of her age, and need so require, let him do what he will, he sinneth not: let them marry. Nevertheless he that standeth stedfast in his heart, having no necessity, but hath power over his own will, and hath so decreed in his heart that he will keep his virgin, doeth well. So then he that giveth her in marriage doeth well; but he that giveth her not in marriage doeth better.”', 'A case with conditions, stated twice: marry and “he sinneth not,” or keep and “doeth well.” The last sentence uses “doeth well” and “doeth better.”'),
], [0.15, 0.48, 0.37]))
A(('p', '6.2 “The present distress” (7:26). Paul’s supposition, “that this is good for the present distress,” ties a judgment to a circumstance. The chapter does not say what the distress is, who is in it, or how long it lasts, and the study does not identify it. The sentence says only that, because of it, “it is good for a man so to be.” The words “so to be” point back to the state of verse 27 (“bound unto a wife” or “loosed from a wife”) and are not defined further.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Verses 27–28: the paired commands. “Art thou bound unto a wife? seek not to be loosed. Art thou loosed from a wife? seek not a wife.” The two sentences are given the same weight, and the next sentence guards against misreading both: “But and if thou marry, thou hast not sinned; and if a virgin marry, she hath not sinned.” Paul adds that “such shall have trouble in the flesh: but I spare you.” The chapter does not say what the trouble is.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Verses 29–31: “the time is short.” Paul says “the time is short: it remaineth,” and then lists five pairs: those with wives as though they had none, those who weep as though they wept not, those who rejoice as though they rejoiced not, those who buy as though they possessed not, those who use the world as not abusing it. The reason stated is “the fashion of this world passeth away.” The passage gives no date and names no event. The study makes no statement about timing and does not tie the phrase to any other passage that speaks of time or of an end.'))
A(('p', '6.5 Verses 32–35: the purpose. Paul states what he would have: “without carefulness.” He states the difference between the unmarried, who care “for the things that belong to the Lord,” and the married, who care “for the things that are of the world.” He applies the difference to the wife and the virgin (7:34). Then he states his own purpose: “for your own profit; not that I may cast a snare upon you,” but for what is “comely,” and so that they may “attend upon the Lord without distraction.”'))
A(('p', '6.6 Verses 36–38: the virgin. The KJV uses “his virgin” (7:36, 37) and “giveth her in marriage” (7:38). The passage does not say who the man is in relation to the virgin; the English words leave it open. The study notes that the verses state two outcomes, each with its condition, and that the closing sentence compares them: “doeth well” and “doeth better.” It does not decide how the man is related to the virgin.'))

A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The widow (7:39). “The wife is bound by the law as long as her husband liveth; but if her husband be dead, she is at liberty to be married to whom she will; only in the Lord.” The statement has three parts: the bond lasts while the husband lives; the widow is “at liberty” to marry; and the one condition is “only in the Lord.” The verse does not say which law is meant.'))
A(('p', '7.2 A parallel in wording. Romans 7:1–3 uses wording close to verse 39: “the woman which hath an husband is bound by the law to her husband so long as he liveth; but if the husband be dead, she is loosed from the law of her husband.” That passage stands inside a different argument (the dominion of the law over a man “as long as he liveth,” 7:1). The study sets the two verses side by side and does not say that either names the same law.'))
A(('p', '7.3 Verse 40. “But she is happier if she so abide, after my judgment: and I think also that I have the Spirit of God.” The closing verse names the authority again: “my judgment.” It compares the widow’s condition (“happier”), and Paul adds a statement about himself. The statement is a conviction, not a command.'))
A(('p', '7.4 The authority markers side by side. The first table gathers the markers in the order they stand, with what follows each.'))
A(('ctbl', '', [
    ['Verse', 'What Paul says of the word', 'What follows'],
    ['7:6', '“by permission, and not of commandment”', 'Duty, consent and the wish that all men were as he is (7:2–9).'],
    ['7:10', '“I command, yet not I, but the Lord”', 'The wife not to depart, the husband not to put away (7:10–11).'],
    ['7:12', '“speak I, not the Lord”', 'The believer and the unbelieving spouse (7:12–16).'],
    ['7:17', '“so ordain I in all churches”', 'Each man abides as called, with two examples (7:17–24).'],
    ['7:25', '“no commandment of the Lord: yet I give my judgment”', 'Virgins, the present distress and the time that is short (7:25–38).'],
    ['7:40', '“after my judgment”', 'The widow’s liberty and the closing sentence (7:39–40).'],
], [0.12, 0.46, 0.42]))
A(('p', '7.5 What the chapter leaves unsaid. The second table lists matters the chapter does not state.'))
A(('ctbl', '', [
    ['Matter', 'What the chapter states', 'What it leaves unsaid'],
    ['The saying of the Lord in 7:10', '“yet not I, but the Lord”', 'Which saying is meant, and whether Paul quotes a Gospel.'],
    ['Paul’s own marital state', '“even as I” (7:7–8)', 'Whether he was ever married; 9:5 asks about a power and states no situation.'],
    ['The present distress', '“good for the present distress” (7:26)', 'What the distress is, who is in it, how long it lasts.'],
    ['“The time is short”', '“the time is short: it remaineth” (7:29)', 'A date, an event or a link to any other passage on time.'],
    ['“Not under bondage”', '“in such cases” (7:15)', 'Remarriage in verses 12–16; what the believer is freed from beyond the verse.'],
    ['“His virgin”', '“his virgin”; “giveth her in marriage” (7:36–38)', 'The relation of the man to the virgin.'],
], [0.26, 0.34, 0.40]))
A(('p', '7.6 Three readings tested. Three readings of the chapter are common, and each can be set beside the words. (a) The chapter commands singleness. Against this stand the markers “by permission, and not of commandment” (7:6) and “no commandment of the Lord” (7:25), and the statements “thou hast not sinned” (7:28) and “he sinneth not” (7:36). (b) The chapter places singleness above marriage. The chapter does use “good,” “better,” “doeth better” and “happier” (7:1, 7:9, 7:38, 7:40), and it uses “better” on both sides (“it is better to marry than to burn”); the reasons it gives are the distress, the cares and the freedom from distraction, and the text does not say these reasons rest on a rank. (c) The chapter’s advice holds only because of the present distress. Verse 26 ties one judgment to the distress, but verses 7, 32–35, 38 and 40 give other reasons, and the text does not say they depend on it. The study sets out what the text says for and against each reading and does not decide among them.'))

A(('h1', 'Conclusion'))
A(('p', 'First Corinthians 7 is an answer to a letter, given in turns, and each turn says what kind of word it is. Paul speaks by permission, commands with the Lord’s authority, speaks as himself, and gives his judgment. The chapter states what is said of duty and consent, of the married and the unmarried, of the believer with an unbelieving spouse, of the rule to abide in the calling, of virgins, the present distress and the time that is short, and of the widow. It also leaves much unsaid: which saying of the Lord is meant, what the distress is, what “not under bondage” frees a believer from, and how the man of verses 36–38 stands to his virgin. This study has reported each instruction in the order given, with its markers, and has set beside the chapter only what the chapter or the other passages state in their own settings.'))

A(('h1', 'Study Summary'))
A(('bul', [
    'Verses 1–9 state duty and consent for both partners, one exception with its conditions, and a word to the unmarried and widows, spoken “by permission, and not of commandment.”',
    'Verses 10–11 give a command Paul says is the Lord’s; Matthew 19 and Mark 10 record the Lord’s words in other settings with other hearers and wording.',
    'Verses 12–16 are Paul’s word, “not the Lord,” for a believer with an unbelieving spouse, and leave several matters unsaid.',
    'Verses 25–38 give “my judgment” on virgins, tied to the present distress and the time that is short, with the statement that marriage is not sin.',
    'Verses 39–40 state the widow’s liberty “only in the Lord” and close with “after my judgment.”',
]))

A(('h1', 'Key Distinctions to Retain'))
A(('bul', [
    'The chapter marks whose word each part is: permission, the Lord’s command, Paul’s own word and Paul’s judgment. The study reports each marker as written.',
    'A statement that something is good is not a statement that something else is sin; verse 28 states that marriage is not sin.',
    'Where the chapter is silent (the Lord’s saying, the distress, remarriage in verses 12–16, the man and his virgin), the study stays silent.',
]))

